WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.310
BRIT CRUISE: When we
perform calculations

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:05.210
with a pen and
paper, we often need

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:07.560
to save intermediate results.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.700
And we may do this
with, say, scrap paper.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.900
And in this case,
the paper is acting

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.370
as a form of external memory.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:20.650
And memory, no matter the
form, takes up physical space.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:22.340
Computers contain memory.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.230
We can think of it as the
scrap paper for the computer.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.590
And say, when you
construct an array

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:32.480
to store values in your
program, you require memory.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:37.330
And at the lowest
level, computers

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:41.940
read and store all instructions
as a string of numbers.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.930
But how do you store
numbers in a machine?

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:48.310
This was a very difficult
problem originally.

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:52.490
Especially when you need
computers to hold their memory

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:55.080
after the access
to power is lost.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.320
This is known as
non-volatile memory.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.520
The easiest difference
for a machine to detect

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:03.810
is simply a presence versus
an absence of something.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.240
And this is how old
punch cards would work.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.110
Along the top, we
have some data.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:11.800
And the vertical
columns contain a series

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.330
of punched holes, which
represent each character.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:24.230
So computers really have
two fingers, base two.

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:29.570
The same as a light switch being
on for one and off for zero.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.980
This is the smallest
amount of information,

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:36.490
a single difference,
which we call a bit.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:38.290
But bits are
powerful for storage

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.320
because the amount
of unique states

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.030
grows exponentially as
we add bits together.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:46.890
Remember one light
switch is one bit

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:49.060
and it can store two states.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:53.290
But two light switches can
store four unique states.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.820
And eight light
switches, or 8 bits,

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:03.590
can store 256 unique states.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:07.320
And space is measured in bits.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.270
But the physical
size of a bit depends

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:12.820
on your method of storage.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:17.040
So how do computer store
zeroes and ones internally?

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.040
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:19.890
SPEAKER 1: Modern data
processing systems

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:24.240
like these use thousands
of magnetic cores.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.100
What are magnetic cores?

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:30.330
They are tiny rings
of nickel alloy

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.920
or other magnetic materials.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.930
They have replaced vacuum tubes
for many important functions

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.287
in data processing systems.

00:02:38.287 --> 00:02:39.120
[END VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.340
BRIT CRUISE: And it allowed
computers to store bits

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:45.190
as clockwise versus
counterclockwise magnetization

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:46.500
direction.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.030
Because each core
could be magnetized

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.150
in two different ways,
depending which direction

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:53.330
the current was applied.

00:03:07.044 --> 00:03:07.710
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:09.820
SPEAKER 1: Because a
bit can be represented

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:12.550
by any bi-stable device.

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:16.287
And the magnetic core
is a bi-stable device.

00:03:16.287 --> 00:03:17.120
[END VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.460
BRIT CRUISE: Later
on, this was done

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.260
using thin film
magnetic disk, where

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.680
we can think of as each a bit
as a tiny magnetic cell, which

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.420
can be charged to store
either a 1 or a 0.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.150
So long story short,
the size of a bit

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:37.280
has been rapidly shrinking
since the days of punch cards.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.890
A hard drive in
a modern computer

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:44.730
can be thought of as billions
of tiny magnetic cells.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.350
Now you may wonder,
well, how small

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:50.390
can these little
magnetic cells be?

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:53.710
And current research
at IBM is pushing this

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:56.060
to the atomic level.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:58.990
Where they have
shown 12 iron atoms

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:02.830
can work together as a
stable magnetic unit.

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.480
Where they're able
to store a 1 or a 0,

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:07.780
depending on how
they're oriented.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.390
And this is approaching
a theoretical limit

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:13.870
where we would hold a
single bit on a single atom.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:25.010
And interestingly
IBM estimates that we

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:29.610
could put around one
quadrillion bits of information

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:34.320
in a handheld device the size
of an iPod with atomic storage.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.190
And let's call
this a super drive.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:37.580
It doesn't even exist yet.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:42.260
As a hypothetical example,
a small handheld super drive

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:48.250
using atomic storage
would hold 1,000 terabits,

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:51.200
which is 1,000
trillion switches.

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:59.300
Or more commonly
known as 125 terabytes

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.910
in the palm of your hand.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:05.070
Or to use an example
everyone can understand,

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:11.720
125 terabytes is the same as
having a 1,250-kilometer-long

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:14.420
bookshelf in the
palm of your hand.

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:17.450
And this is what the future
of memory looks like.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:19.660
Will we ever be able to
store a bit on something

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:22.170
smaller than an atom?

